!! Environment selects the command listener adapter

At this point, we don't want ==EDASSettingsPort== to provide the adapter for the command listener port. Instead, we want it to be derived by the environment adapter.
If the environment is manually specified, the command listener adapter should be the ''InMemory'' one. If it's chosen from a list, the command listener settings will be read-only, and retrieved from a JSON file.

!!! Removing the command listener port

The command listener port cannot be chosen manually anymore. We need to remove that port in the Ports section of the settings.

% ${changes:path=code/001-EDASSettings_portSettingsOn_under_on_order_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> portSettingsOn: aBuilder under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	| entry environmentValues key portResolver commandListenerValues |
	key := aParent , 'ports'.
	entry := aBuilder group: key.
	entry
		label: 'Ports' translated;
		parent: aParent.
	portResolver := EDASPortResolver new.
	environmentValues := OrderedCollection withAll:
		                     ((portResolver
			                       availableEnvironmentSettingsAdapters
			                       collect: [ :adapterClass |
			                       adapterClass description translated
			                       -> adapterClass ]) sorted: [ :a :b | a < b ]).
	(aBuilder pickOne: #environmentSettingsAdapterClass)
		label: 'Environment adapter' translated;
		target: app portSettings;
		parent: key;
		domainValues: environmentValues;
		order: 1.
	commandListenerValues := OrderedCollection withAll:
		                         ((portResolver
			                           availableCommandListenerSettingsAdapters
			                           collect: [ :adapterClass |
			                           adapterClass description translated
			                           -> adapterClass ]) sorted: [ :a :b |
			                          a < b ])
]]]

!!! Binding the command listener in-memory adapter to the environment in-memory adapter

First things first. We'll leverage the announcements we prepared previously to implement this logic. The new event would be ==EDASEnvironmentChanged==.

% ${changes:path=code/002-EDASEnvironmentChanged.ombu}
[[[classDefinition=true
EDASSettingsChanged subclass: #EDASEnvironmentChanged
	instanceVariableNames: 'oldAdapterClass newAdapterClass'
	classVariableNames: ''
	package: 'EDAS-Settings'
]]]

In ==EDASEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter== we can now emit the event if the setting actually changes.

% ${changes:path=code/003-EDASEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter_currentEnvironment_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
{ 'category': 'accessing' }
EDASEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter >> currentEnvironment: anObject

	| oldValue |
	currentEnvironment ~= anObject ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
	oldValue := currentEnvironment.
	currentEnvironment := anObject.
	self edaApplication
		notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: oldValue
		to: anObject
]]]

The same applies to =EDASPredefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter=.
% ${changes:path=code/004-EDASPredefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter_currentEnvironment_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
{ 'category': 'accessing' }
EDASPredefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter >> currentEnvironment: anObject

	| oldValue |
	currentEnvironment ~= anObject ifFalse: [ ^ self ].
	oldValue := currentEnvironment.
	currentEnvironment := anObject.
	self edaApplication
		notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: oldValue
		to: anObject
]]]

As you can see, we are relying upon the ==EDAApplication== instance to do it, because it has a reference to the ==EDASSettingsAnnouncer== instance, lazily initialized in its ''settingsAnnouncer:'' accessor.

% ${changes:path=code/005-EDAApplication_notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom_to_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
{ 'category': 'events' }
EDAApplication >> notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: oldValue to: newValue

	self settingsAnnouncer announce:
		(EDASEnvironmentChanged old: oldValue new: newValue on: self)
]]]

Recall that ''setupListeners'' in ==EDASSettingsAnnouncer== finds out any class that uses ==EDASSettingsChangedListener== trait. Therefore, to react upon the new event we just need to create a class using that trait.

% ${changes:path=code/006-EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter.ombu}
[[[classDefinition=true
Object subclass: #EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter
	uses: EDASSettingsChangedListener
	instanceVariableNames: ''
	classVariableNames: ''
	package: 'EDAS-Settings'
]]]

To make sure we're reacting upon =EDASEnvironmentChanged=, let's just reset the command listener settings adapter whenever the environment changes.

% ${changes:path=code/007-EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter_class_subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter class >> subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn: anEDASettingsAnnouncer

	anEDASettingsAnnouncer when: EDASEnvironmentChanged do: [ :event |
    	event edaApplication portSettings commandListenerSettings:
			(EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsInMemoryAdapter withApp: event edaApp) ] ]
]]]

So far, if we change some of the command listener settings and afterwards we change the environment, those modifications are gone.

!!! Updating the command listener settings based on the environment

Now we are in position to update the command listener settings using the environment-specific values, available in the associated json files.

Let's implement this, regardless of whether the command listener settings port is the json adapter or not. We'll deal with that later.

We're missing a method to build a json adapter based on the environment value.

% ${changes:path=code/008-EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter_class_fromEnvironment_withApp_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter class forEnvironment: anEnvironment withApp: anEDAApplication

	^ self fromFile:
		  FileSystem workingDirectory / 'config' / (anEnvironment , '.json') withApp: anEDAApplication
]]]

% ${changes:path=code/009-EDASEnvironmentChangedListener_class_subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentChangedListener class >> subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn: anEDASettingsAnnouncer

	anEDASettingsAnnouncer when: EDASEnvironmentChanged do: [ :event |
    	event edaApplication portSettings commandListenerSettings:
			(EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsInMemoryAdapter forEnvironment: event newValue withApp: event edaApp) ] ]
]]]

Additionally, it'd be helpful if the json adapter can provide us a way to retrieve the available environments.

% ${changes:path=code/010-EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter_class_availableEnvironments.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter class >> availableEnvironments

	| helper |
	helper := EDACSourceCodeHelperStub new.
	^ ((FileSystem workingDirectory / 'config') files
		   select: [ :f | f pathString endsWith: '.json' ]
		   thenCollect: [ :f |
		   helper replace: '.json' with: '' inString: f asPath basename ])
		  reversed
]]]

The ''reversed'' part has to do with the convention of using upper-cased names for expressing its importance, i.e., we'd use ==dev== instead of ==DEV==, ==PRO== instead of ==pro==.

We can also update ==EDASPeedefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter=='s ==availableEnvironments== to use the code above as well.

% ${changes:path=code/011-EDASPredefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter_class_availableEnvironments.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASPredefinedEnvironmentSettingsInMemoryAdapter class >> availableEnvironments

	^ EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter availableEnvironments
]]]

To finish this part, we could even switch to the json adapter whenever the environment the user specifies is one of the pre-existing ones, regardless of the environment settings adapter.

% ${changes:path=code/012-EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter_class_subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn_.ombu}
[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASEnvironmentSwitchesToCommandListenerJsonAdapter class >> subscribeToSettingsChangedEventsIn: anEDASettingsAnnouncer

	anEDASettingsAnnouncer when: EDASEnvironmentChanged do: [ :event |
		(EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter supportsEnvironment:
			 event newValue)
			ifTrue: [
				event edaApplication portSettings commandListenerSettings:
					(EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsJsonAdapter
						 forEnvironment: event newValue
						 withApp: event edaApplication) ]
			ifFalse: [
				event edaApplication portSettings commandListenerSettings:
					(EDASStompTopicListenerSettingsInMemoryAdapter withApp: event edaApplication) ] ]
]]]

However, when launching a new SettingsBrowser, we notice the environment is retrieved from the list of JSON files, but the command listener adapter is not the JSON's.
One easy way to fix this is to emit an event when building the settings tree.

[[[methodDefinition=true
EDASSettings class >> environmentSettingsOn: aBuilder under: aParent on: app order: anInteger

	self
		settingOn: aBuilder
		named: #currentEnvironment
		labelled: 'Environment'
		withDescription: 'Environment'
		usingConfig: app environmentSettings
		under: aParent
		on: app
		order: anInteger.
	app
		notifyEnvironmentChangedFrom: nil
		to: app environmentSettings currentEnvironment
]]]
